.TH RUN-MAILBOX-CLEANUP 1 "2026-10-12" "cti-agent 1.3" "CTI Fleet Manual"
.SH NAME
run-mailbox-cleanup \- daily tidy of the CTI mailbox: archive what was processed, delete auto-replies
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B run\-mailbox\-cleanup
.RB [ \-\-for\-real ]
.PP
.B sudo cti\-agent run\-mailbox\-cleanup
.RB [ \-\-for\-real ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B run-mailbox-cleanup
runs
.BR cti\-mailbox (1)
against the shared CTI mailbox and reports the result on the board.
.B cti\-mailbox
moves advisories the digest has a record of reading to Archive, moves
header-confirmed out-of-office replies to Deleted Items, and leaves
everything else where it is. It refuses to act on a day with no processed
CTI email, and that refusal is a success.
.PP
It is a dry run unless
.B \-\-for\-real
is given. This is the only lane that changes the mailbox, so the effect has
to be asked for: a unit file that loses its argument does nothing.
.PP
It is run by
.B cti\-agent\-mailbox.service
as
.B run\-mailbox\-cleanup \-\-for\-real
from
.BR cti\-agent\-mailbox.timer ,
every day at 07:00, an hour after the digest that writes the
processed-message log it depends on. The timer is not
.BR Persistent :
a cleanup missed while the box was off is not run at boot. The unit runs as
.BR ctiagent ,
with a 10-minute start timeout and
.B cti\-agent\-alert@.service
on failure.
.PP
The steps, in order:
.TP
.B 1.
Checks that
.I $FLEET_CODE/bin/cti\-mailbox
is executable; fatal if not.
.TP
.B 2.
Runs
.B cti\-mailbox
.RB [ \-\-for\-real ]
.RB [ \-\-backlog\-threshold
.IR N ]
.B \-\-json
.IR plan ,
capturing its standard output and standard error together. The output is
printed once the binary finishes. A non-zero exit is fatal; it usually means
the app registration lacks
.B Mail.ReadWrite
with admin consent.
.TP
.B 3.
Posts the binary's summary line (archive, delete and leave counts), or
"nothing to do", to the board for
.BR @you ,
marked
.B DRY RUN
when it was one.
.TP
.B 4.
If the binary reported an unread backlog at or above the threshold (mail
with no processing record), posts that line as a separate WARN: it is the
sign that the digest stopped reading the mailbox.
.PP
A fatal step prints
.B FAILED:
and posts an ERROR line to the board before exiting 1.
.PP
The dry run reads the mailbox, writes the plan file and posts the board
line. It moves and deletes nothing.
.SH OPTIONS
A shell argument, matched exactly. Any other argument is refused with a
usage line and exit status 2.
.TP
.B \-\-for\-real
Pass
.B \-\-for\-real
to
.BR cti\-mailbox ,
which then moves the mail its plan lists. Without it nothing is moved.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.I fleet.env
is sourced with every assignment exported, and its values replace the
process environment, except
.B FLEET_HOME
and
.BR FLEET_CODE ,
which keep the value the unit or wrapper injected.
.TP
.BR FLEET_HOME ", " FLEET_CODE ", " FLEET_ENV
Fleet layout, as for
.BR run\-digest (1).
.TP
.B FLEET_MAILBOX_BACKLOG_THRESHOLD
When set and non-empty, passed as
.BR \-\-backlog\-threshold ;
when empty, the binary's own default applies (section 4, "mailbox
cleanup").
Default:\~\fB25\fR.
.PP
Graph credentials and the cleanup window (sections 1 and 10) are read by
.BR cti\-mailbox (1).
.SH FILES
Under
.IR /var/lib/cti\-agent/state .
.TP
.I mailbox\-cleanup\-DATE.json
The plan:
what was archived, deleted and left, and why. 0600. Written on dry and real
runs alike, but not when the binary refuses because no CTI email was
processed today; the board line names the path either way.
.I DATE
is the local date, so a later run the same day replaces it.
.TP
.I processed\-messages.json
The digest's record of which messages it read, and the only authority
.B cti\-mailbox
has to move one.
.TP
.I /var/lib/cti\-agent/board.md
The fleet board.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
.B cti\-mailbox
succeeded, including when it decided to do nothing.
.TP
.B 1
.B cti\-mailbox
is missing or exited non-zero, for example on a failed move or an
unreadable processed-message log.
.TP
.B 2
An unknown argument.
.SH EXAMPLES
See what tomorrow's cleanup would move:
.PP
.EX
sudo cti\-agent run\-mailbox\-cleanup
sudo less /var/lib/cti\-agent/state/mailbox\-cleanup\-$(date +%F).json
.EE
.PP
Run the real cleanup by hand:
.PP
.EX
sudo cti\-agent run\-mailbox\-cleanup \-\-for\-real
.EE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR cti\-mailbox (1),
.BR run\-digest (1),
.BR fleet\-board (1),
.BR cti\-agent (1)
.PP
RUNBOOK.md section 10; fleet\-kit/README.md, "Mailbox cleanup".
